A||N||2||Ozzy||Exhaust questions...||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||03-27-2000||12:40 AM||||Hey there...<P>Sure feels strange to be posting questions on a 6cyl Fiero!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Guess I'll get over it.<P>So, on the rear manifold (closest to passenger compartment) there exists a bloody great hole almost the whole way around to the right of the bolted flange. (Sounds kinda like a Cessna) <P>I've read some older posts from a search on this subject and it seems that these manifolds are somewhat problematic. Somebody has already tried to weld/repair this one and clearly could not weld worth a s***!<BR>So can you guys tell me what you have done in this case. What is the best fix without spending a fortune? Can these be reinforced sucessfully? <P>Also, I know that the hole will allow most of the pressure to exit at that point but I feel very little exhaust gas coming from the tailpipes. Is it common to have a clogged cat. converter??  There are no smog restrictions where I live. Can I remove the Cat. completely??<P>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||03-27-2000||01:16 AM||||I had my manifold welded once and a year later it cracked in a different spot. I just bought the Sprint Manifolds and haven't had a problem since. (expensive but worth it to me) If you do not have to have your vehicle smog tested and It is legal in your state, you can remove it. Here in Illinois, It is legal for the owner of the car to remove the smog devices. The catch is The owner has to remove them. He cannot pay someone to remove them. The big catch is you still have to pass the emission testing for your particular year of the car. Most cars will not pass if the cat is removed. If you fail and they find you do not have a cat they can make you put one on. I know someone who has his stock exaust system and an aftermarket system without a cat. If they call him for testing in 45 minutes he swaps exhausts gets tested and passes then home again and swaps back!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||03-27-2000||05:34 AM||||Yes it is common for cats to clog up.<BR>How much time do you have and how is your welding?  Things can always be fixed, but since it is already boogered time can be extraordinarily long to fix it correctly, lotsa grinding and fitting.<BR>New parts are quicktime, long on money though.||||reg||12
